alfe needs an option — like --dry-run, but concrete — that prints the
AutoCAD / BricsCAD command line it WOULD use.

It should prepare the workdir as usual, and erase it unless
--keep-workdir is given.

(It is understood that running that command in bash, even with the
workdir kept, would just launch bricscad or autocad or accoreconsole,
and could not go very far without alfe processing its side of the
protocol. For now that is OK — the value is seeing the exact binary,
switches and script path.)

pjb: --dry-run is already taken; use --print-command.


* Resolution (feature-alfe-print-command, alfe 1.7.10 / clautolisp 1.7.6)

New alfe option --print-command. It is NOT a variant of --dry-run:

  --dry-run        resolves the backend by NAME only, touches no disk,
                   prints the action plan.
  --print-command  resolves the backend WITH detection, stages the
                   workdir for real, prints the argv, launches nothing.

Contract:

- Real staging. prepare-workdir + start-engine run exactly as in
  run-plan: runtime and bootstrap LSP staged, run-common.lsp emitted,
  run.scr (or bridge-bricscad.vbs / launcher.applescript under
  --mode automation) written. So the printed line is the real one,
  binary path included.

- No spawn. The argv is captured by handing START-ENGINE a :launcher
  closure that records its argument and returns NIL — the same seam
  the test suite uses for the mock CAD — with :wait-for-ready NIL. No
  SHUTDOWN is attempted either: nothing was launched, and BricsCAD's
  SHUTDOWN would spend its 5 s waiting for a STOPPED status that
  cannot arrive.

- One line on stdout: the command alone, each argument quoted for the
  host shell (POSIX single-quote rules; cmd.exe double quotes when the
  host OS is Windows), so `cmd=$(alfe --print-command ...)' works.
  Backend / workdir / raw argv go to the log at :verbose / :debug.

- Cleanup: the workdir is removed on the way out unless
  --keep-workdir. --write-workdir-path FILE still records its path.

- CAD backends only. Under --clautolisp the engine runs in-process and
  there is no external command line, so it is a CLI-USAGE-ERROR
  (exit 2) rather than a silent empty answer — a caller capturing the
  output fails loudly.

Examples:

  $ alfe --bricscad --print-command -x "(princ)"
  /opt/bricsys/bricscad/bricscad -B /tmp/alfe-bricscad-31337-a1b2c3/run.scr

  $ alfe --bricscad --print-command --keep-workdir -x "(princ)"
  '/opt/fake cad/bricscad v26' -B /tmp/alfe-bricscad-1617099-25f79e/run.scr

Implementation:

- clautolisp/autolisp-cli/source/options.lisp — new print-command-p
  slot on the shared cli-options struct (alfe-only), exported from
  clautolisp.autolisp-cli.
- autolisp-front-end/source/cli.lisp — option spec, help text,
  shell-quote-argument / windows-quote-argument /
  quote-command-argument / format-launch-command, and
  print-command-plan (the staged-no-spawn driver).

Tests: 27 new FiveAM checks (alfe suite 434 -> 461, 100% pass):
parsing + POSIX/Windows quoting + the non-CAD usage error in
cli-tests.lisp; end-to-end staging in backend-cad-tests.lisp
(bricscad-print-command-plan-prints-argv-and-erases-workdir asserts a
single line naming the binary and run.scr and that the workdir is
gone; ...-keep-workdir-leaves-staged-artefacts asserts run.scr and
run-common.lsp survive). Conformance scenario
cli/print-command.sexp (37 pass, 0 fail); spec-coverage 33/33 —
--write-workdir-path was pulled into the coverage set by the new spec
prose and is now covered by cli/option-coverage.sexp.

Documented in alfe-user-manual.org (§ "Seeing the CAD command line"),
alfe--specifications.org (§ "--print-command (staged launch, no
spawn)") and RELEASE_NOTES.org.
